AN ITEM THE AUTHOR WROTE EMPTY IS STILL AN ITEM.

Three bullets in, three out. `format` used to give back two, and it writes the
file, so the middle line was gone for good after one run. On a checklist that
is an errand nobody will do, because nothing is left to say it was ever there.

`-` IS WRITTEN BARE, with no trailing space. The space after a marker
SEPARATES it from the content, and there is no content here to separate it
from. `- ` would leave whitespace at the end of the line.

A lone checkbox is content and keeps both spaces, its own and the marker's:
`- [ ] ` is right, and `format-keeps-an-item-that-is-only-a-checkbox` pins it.
The two cases are the same branch of the writer asking whether anything
follows the marker, so change one and run the other.

CAUSE, while it was broken. `MarkdownParser.collectListItems` drops a flush
that gathered nothing:

    guard !text.isEmpty || task != nil else { return }

That guard is right about the pieces a nested list splits off — an item whose
text runs out before the nested list has nothing to emit — but it could not
tell those apart from an item that was empty to begin with. An item the author
wrote empty has NO CHILDREN, which a mid-item flush cannot say, so the guard
now asks that question too.

THE TRAP THAT MAKES THIS EXCEPTION NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS.
`format-keeps-a-link-reference-definition-in-a-list-item` is a known-fail
because `- [ref]: /url` ALSO has no children: cmark consumes a link reference
definition into the document's reference map and leaves no node behind. So
this fix gives that input a bare `-` and its URL stays lost. That is an
improvement — before, the entire list block vanished — but it is not the end
of it, and the source line is the only thing that can tell an item written
empty from one whose definition was eaten.
